message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Attention-Mass Condensation for Sparse Decoding: Margin Stability, Query-Dependent Retrieval, and Operating Limits"
authors:
  - family-names: "Ruiz Williams"
    given-names: "Jorge L."
type: "software"
date-released: 2026-08-10
repository-code: "https://github.com/JorgeLRW/condensate-theorem"
license: "MIT"
keywords:
  - "attention mechanism"
  - "sparse attention"
  - "sparse decoding"
  - "KV cache"
  - "transformer"
abstract: |
  Attention-mass concentration creates an opportunity for sparse decoding, but
  retained mass alone does not guarantee a stable greedy decision. This work
  gives an exact omitted-mass identity and a sufficient downstream margin
  condition (the Condensation Principle), then tests the principle with a
  query-dependent mean-pooled block selector. On Qwen2-0.5B, none of 60 paired
  runs remains identical to dense decoding through 128 tokens, while
  distributional quality depends on context length and support size.
